The Medallion Architecture is a data design pattern used to logically organize and manage data within a Lakehouse.
It guarantees a structured, predictable pipeline by progressively improving data quality as it flows through three distinct layers: Bronze, Silver, and Gold."

Technical Breakdown :-
A. Bronze Layer (Raw): This is the landing zone. We ingest data directly from source systems (APIs, logs, relational databases) in its raw, native state.
It is typically append-only and serves as our immutable historical archive. If downstream transformations fail, we can always replay from Bronze.

B. Silver Layer (Validated): This is the 'Enterprise View.' Here, we take the Bronze data and apply data quality checks, filtering, deduplication, and schema enforcement.
The data is normalized and cleaned, making it the perfect playground for Data Scientists and machine learning models.

C. Gold Layer (Curated): This is the 'Business View.' We take the Silver data and apply complex business logic, aggregations, and joins.
The data is highly refined and usually structured into star schemas. It is strictly optimized for BI tools, dashboards, and executive reporting."

Buisness Impact :- 
From a business perspective, the Medallion architecture prevents the classic 'data swamp' problem.
It ensures that different personas get precisely what they need: Data Engineers manage the pipeline, Data Scientists get clean but unaggregated data in Silver, and Business Analysts get highly performant, trustworthy metrics in Gold.

While the concept is straightforward, the real power unlocks when you pair it with modern open table formats like Delta Lake or Apache Iceberg.
This allows us to enforce strict ACID transactions and schema evolution at every boundary.
If a bad record hits the pipeline, we can quarantine it between Bronze and Silver without breaking the executive dashboard sitting on the Gold layer.
